\id 1JN Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 1 John
\toc1 The First Epistle of John the Apostle.
\toc2 1 John
\toc3 1JN
\mt1 The First Epistle of John the Apostle.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF S. JOHN'S THREE EPISTLES.
\ip OF S. John was said in the Argument before his Gospel. Now here follow his three Epistles: one to all Catholics (though \f - \ft \+it * Higinus ep. 1. to. 1. Concil. August. li. 2. Evang. quaest. q. 39.\+it*\f* some ancients do call it, \it Ad Parthos\it* :) the other two being very short, unto a certain Lady, & to one Gaius. The effect of all is, to witness unto them the certainty of the Catholic faith, and to exhort them to continue still in it: also to love the Catholic Church, and so, neither to become heretics, nor Schismatics: but rather to avoid all such, as the forerunners of Antichrist, and to remember, that Catholics need not to go to school to any such Masters, having at home in the Catholic Church, the doctrine of the Holy Ghost himself, who was given to the Church visibly in the beginning, to lead her into all truth, and to continue with her for ever. Therefore he saith: \f - \ft \+it 1. Ioh. 2. v. 24.\+it*\f* \it That which you have heard from the beginning, let it abide in you.\it* Likewise a little after. v. 27. and ep. 2. v. 6. \it This is the commandment, that as you have heard from the beginning, you walk in the same, because many seducers are gone out into the world.\it* and v. 8. & 9.
\ip And not only thus in general, but also in particular he expresseth the points which the heretics did then call in question. Some were about Christ himself. For they denied that JESUS is Christ, that he is the very son of God, that he is incarnate. Ep. 1. c. 2. v. 22. and Ep. 2. v. 7. And against such it was that he wrote his Gospel also, as he there signifieth. Ioh. 20. v. 31. Other points are about our justification, against only faith, and for good works, as also S. Aug. noted \f - \ft \+it De fid. & op. c. 14.\+it*\f* , whose words were cited before. Hereupon he saith: \it If we say we have society with God, and walk in darkness, we lie.\it* Ep. 1. c. 1. Again, \f - \ft \+it 1. Ioh. 2. & 5.\+it*\f* \it He that saith he knoweth God, and keepeth not his commandments, is a liar.\it* Again, \it This is the charity of God, that we keep his commandments, and his commandments are not heavy.\it* Finally, \it Children let no man seduce you, he that doth justice, is just, even as he is just.\it* Ep. 1. c. 3. v. 7. 8. 9. likewise c. 2. v. 29. and indeed in all the three Epistles throughout, he doth inculcate good works and keeping the commandments, against the heresy of only faith.
\c 1
\cd Good cause there is to believe the Apostles' preaching. 5. And this is one point of their preaching, that to have participation with God, we must not only believe, but also abstain from all mortal sin, 8. though we all sin venially.
\p
\v 1 \sc That\sc* which was from the beginning, which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon, and our hands have handled, of the word of life:
\v 2 (and the life was manifested: and we have seen, and do testify, and declare unto you the life eternal which was with the Father, and hath appeared to us)
\v 3 that which we have seen and have heard, we declare unto you, that you also may have society with us, and our society may be with the Father and with his Son \sc Jesus\sc* Christ. \ef - \fr 1.3 \fq You may have society. \ft ¹ S. John sheweth manifestly, that whosoever desire to be partakers with God, must first be united to the Church's society, learn that faith, and receive those Sacraments, which the Disciples received of the Truth it-self, conversant with them in flesh. So saith Venerable Bede upon this place. Whereby we see there is no society with God in sects or schisms, nor any-where but in the unity, fellowship, and communion of that Church which can prove it-self to descend from the Apostles. \fq ¹ \ft No salvation but in the Society of the Church.\ef*
\v 4 And these things we write to you, that you may rejoice, and your joy may be full.
\v 5 And this is the annunciation which we have heard of him, and declare unto you, That \x - \xt Io. 8, 12.\x* God is light, and in him there is no darkness:
\v 6 If we shall say that we have society with him, and walk in darkness, we lie, and do not the truth.
\v 7 But if we walk in the light, as he also is in the light: we have society one toward another, and \x - \xt Heb. 9. 1. Pet. 1. Apo. 1.\x* the blood of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin. \ef - \fr 1.7 \fq The blood of Jesus. \ft ¹ Whether sins be remitted by prayers, by fasting, by alms, by faith, by charity, by sacrifice, by Sacraments, & by the Priests, (as the holy Scriptures do plainly attribute remission to every of these) yet none of all these do otherwise remit, but in the force, by the merit & virtue of Christ's blood: these being but the appointed means & instruments by which Christ will have his holy blood to work effectually in us. which means whosoever contemneth, depriveth himself of the commodity of Christ's own blood, & continueth still in sin and uncleanness, vaunt he himself never so much of Christ's death. Which point let the Protestants mark well, and cease to beguile their poor deceived followers, persuading them, that the Catholics derogate from Christ's blood, or seek remission otherwise than by it, for that they use humbly the means appointed by Christ to apply the benefit of his holy blood unto them. \fq ¹ \ft Many means & instruments of remitting sin, but all by the force & merit of Christ's blood applied by them.\ef* \ef - \fr 1.7 \fq From all sin. \ft ¹ From original and actual, venial and mortal, \+it a culpa & poena\+it*, that is, from the fault and the pain due for the same. V. Bede saith, that Christ's Passion doth not only remit in Baptism the sins before committed, but all other afterward also done by frailty: yet so, if we use for the remission of them, such means as be requisite and as Christ hath appointed, whereof he reckoneth some. \+it Bede upon this place.\+it* See S. Augustine also upon this place \+it to. 9.\+it* and S. Hierome \+it li. 2 cont. Pelag. c. 3.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft All remission of sins is by the Passion of Christ though by secondary means also.\ef*
\v 8 \x - \xt 3. Reg. 8, 46. 2. Par. 6, 36.\x* If we shall say that we have no sin, we seduce ourselves, and the truth is not in us. \ef - \fr 1.8 \fq That we have no sin. \ft ¹ We gather by these words and the former, that there be two sorts of sins: one mortal, excluding us from light and the society of God: another venial, which is found even in those that walk in the light, and are in the society of God. ² Also we note against the Pelagians, that we be truly called the sons of God, and so just indeed, though we be not without all sins, every one of us, as well just as unjust, being taught and bound to confess our offences, and to ask pardon daily of God, by this petition of the \+it Pater noster, Forgive us our debts\+it*. Therefore S. Augustine \+it li. de natura & grat. c. 36.\+it* reckoneth up all the holy Patriarchs, Prophets, and renowned just persons, to have been sinners, even when they were in grace and justice: ³ excepting always Our B. Lady, \+it de qua propter honorem Domini, nullam prorsus, cum de peccatis agitur, habere volo quæstionem. of whom,\+it* saith he, \+it for the honour of our Lord, when we talk of sins, I will have no question.\+it* And Pelagius asking what sins Abel & such just men did commit, ⁴ ⁵ S. Augustine answereth, that they might laugh sometime immoderately, or jest too much, or covet somewhat intemperately, or pluck fruit over greedily, or in eating take somewhat more than afterward was well digested, or have their intention in time of prayer somewhat distracted, and such like. thus in sense S. Augustine. Whereby we may learn which be venial sins, that consist with true justice & ⁶ cannot always be avoided even of holy men in this life. In the book \+it de fide ad Petrum c. 41.\+it* are excepted from this common rule of sinners, the children which be newly baptized and have not yet use of reason to sin either mortally or venially. \fq ¹ \ft Some sins venial. \fq ² \ft A man may be truly just, notwithstanding venial sins. \fq ³ \ft S. Augustine excepteth our B. Lady from sinners. \fq ⁴ \ft Examples of venial sins. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it * c. 38.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it * de dono perseverant. c. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 9 If we confess our sins, he is faithful & just, for to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all iniquity.
\v 10 If we shall say that we have not sinned, we make him a liar, and his word is not in us.
\c 2
\cd If any sin mortally, he must not despair. 3. To know God rightly, is not to believe only, but to keep his commandments: 7. and that this is no new doctrine, but the very primitive, though a new life it is. 9. Therefore he that believeth must also love his brethren: 12. and that man must not love the world, but do that which God willeth. 18. Many are gone out of the Church and become Seducers, all the ministers of Antichrist: but true Christians must continue in their old faith, considering the reward, and that they need not go to school to any Heretic, the Holy Ghost himself being the schoolmaster of the Church. 29. He doth earnestly inculcate justice and good works.
\p
\v 1 My little children, these things I write to you, that you sin not. But and if any man shall sin, we have an \f 1 \fr 2.1 \ft \+it c παράκλητον.\+it*\f* Advocate with the Father, \sc Jesus\sc* Christ the just: \ef - \fr 2.1 \fq That you sin not. \ft S. John (saith V. Bede upon this place) is not contrary to himself, in that he seeketh here to make them without sin, whom he said in the last chapter could not be without all sins. but in the former place he warned us only of our frailty, that we should not arrogate to ourselves perfect innocency: here he provoketh us to watchfulness and diligence in resisting and avoiding sins, specially the greater, which by God's grace may more easily be repelled.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.1 \fq An advocate. \ft ¹ The calling and office of an Advocate, is in many things proper to Christ, and in every condition more singularly and excellently agreeing to him than to any Angel, Saint, or creature living, though these also be rightly and truly so called, & that not only without all derogation, but much to the honour of Christ's advocation. To him solely and only it agreeth to procure us mercy before God's face, by the general ransom, price, & payment of his blood for our delivery, as is said in the sentence following, \+it And he is the propitiation for our sins, and not for ours only, but for the whole worlds\+it*. In which sort he is our only advocate, because he is our only redeemer. and hereupon he alone immediately, by and through himself, and without the aid or assistance of any other, man or Angel. in his own name, right, and merits, confidently dealeth in our causes before God our judge, & so procureth our pardon, which is the highest degree of advocation that can be. ² \fq ¹ \ft How Christ is our only Advocate. \fq ² \ft How Angels, Saints, & men alive are our advocates.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.1 \ft All which notwithstanding, yet the Angels, and Saints, and our fellows alive, may and do pray for us, and in that they deal with God by intercession to procure mercy for us, may justly be called our advocates: not so as Christ is, who demandeth all things immediately by his own merits, but as secondary intercessors, who never ask nor obtain any thing for us, but \+it per Christum Dominum nostrum\+it*, by and through Christ our common Lord, Advocate, and Redeemer of mankind. And behold how S. Augustine ( \+it tract. 1. in ep. Io.\+it* upon these very words) prevented the Heretics' cavillations, \+it Sed dicet aliquis, &c. But some man will say, Do not the Saints then pray for us? Do not Bishops then or Prelates and Pastors pray for the people? Yes,\+it* saith he: \+it Mark the Scriptures, and you shall find that the Apostles prayed for the people, and again desired the people to pray for them, and so the head prayeth for all, and the members one for another\+it*. ¹ And likwise (lest the Heretics should say, there is a difference betwixt the living, and the dead in this case) thus the same holy father writeth upon the 85. Psalm in fine. \+it Our Lord Jesus Christ doth yet make intercession for us, all the Martyrs that be with him, pray for us: neither will their intercession cease, till we cease our groanings.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Saints in heaven pray for us.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.1 \ft In this sense therefore whosoever prayeth for us, either alive or dead, is our advocate, as S. Augustine ( \+it ep. 59. to Paulinus circa med.\+it* ) calleth Bishops, the people's advocates, when they give them their benediction or blessing. ¹ So doth the holy Church call our B. Lady our advocate, by the very words of S. Irenæus ², that you may see such speeches be no new inventions of the later ages, but Apostolical. \+it The obedient Virgin \+sc Marie\+sc*\+it* (saith he) \+it is made the advocate of the disobedient virgin Eve\+it*. And to confound the Protestants plainly, in that they think or pretend that the advocation or patronage of Saints should be injurious to Christ, remember that ³ our Saviour acknowledgeth Angels to be deputed for the protection (which is nothing else but advocation) of infants before the face of God, besides the plain examples in the old Testament \+it Gen. 48. v. 16. Tob. 5. v. 27. & c. 12. v. 12. Dan. 10.\+it* ⁴ And this not only the Catholic Church, but the very English Protestants themselves in their service book and in the Collect of Michaelmas day, profess, and pray for the same protection or advocation of Angels, and defend the same against their younger brethren the Puritanes. \fq ¹ \ft The B. Virgin is our advocate. \fq ² \ft \+it Iren. li. 3. c. 33. & li. 5. post med.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it D. Hiero. in Mat. c. 18.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft Angels are our protectors.\ef*
\v 2 and he is the propitiation for our sins: and not for ours only, but also for the whole world's. \ef - \fr 2.2 \fq For the whole worlds. \ft ¹ S. Augustine gathereth hereof against the Donatists, and all other Heretics, that would drive the Church into corners or some certain countries, from the universality of all Nations (whereof it was named by the Apostles, Catholic) that the true religion, and Church, and consequently the effects of Christ's propitiation, death, and advocation, pertaineth not to one age, nation, or people, but to the whole world. S. Augustine upon this place \+it to. 9. tract. 1 in ep. Io.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The Catholic Church is the only true Church.\ef*
\v 3 And in this we know that we have known him, if we observe his commandments.
\v 4 He that saith he knoweth him, and keepeth not his commandments, is a liar, and the truth is not in him: \ef - \fr 2.4 \fq He that saith he knoweth. \ft ¹ To know God here, signifieth (as it doth often in the Scriptures) to love, that is, as in the last chapter, to be in society with him, and to have familiar and experimental knowledge of his graces. If any vaunt himself thus to know God, and yet keepeth not his commandments, he is a liar, as all Calvinists and Lutherans, that profess themselves to be in the favour of God by only faith: affirming, that they neither keep, nor possibly can keep his commandments. \fq ¹ \ft Not only faith.\ef*
\v 5 But he that keepeth his word, in him in very deed the charity of God is perfited: in this we know that we be in him.
\v 6 He that saith he abideth in him, ought even as he walked, himself also to walk.
\v 7 My dearest, I write not a new commandment to you, but an old commandment which you had from the beginning. The old commandment is the word which you have heard.
\v 8 Again \x - \xt Io. 13, 34. 15, 12.\x* a new commandment write I to you, which thing is true both in him and in you: because the darkness is passed, and the true light now shineth.
\v 9 He that saith he is in the light, and hateth his brother, is in the darkness even until now.
\v 10 \x - \xt 1. Io. 3, 14.\x* He that loveth his brother, abideth in the light, and scandal is not in him.
\v 11 But he that hateth his brother, is in the darkness, and walketh in the darkness, and knoweth not whither he goeth, because the darkness hath blinded his eyes.
\v 12 I write unto you little children, because your sins are forgiven you for his name.
\v 13 I write unto you fathers, because you have known him which is from the beginning. I write unto you young men, because you have overcome the wicked one.
\v 14 I write to you infants, because you have known the Father. I write unto you young men, because you are strong, and the word of God abideth in you, and you have overcome the wicked one.
\v 15 Love not the world, nor those things which are in the world. If any man love the world, the charity of the Father is not in him.
\v 16 because \f 1 \fr 2.16 \ft How all sin & tentation proceed of these three, see S. Thomas in his Sum. 1. 2. \+it quæst. 77. art. 5.\+it*\f* all that is in the world, is the concupiscence of the flesh, and the concupiscence of the eyes, and the pride of life, which is not of the Father, but is of the world.
\v 17 And the world passeth and the concupiscence thereof. But he that doeth the will of God, abideth for ever.
\v 18 Little children, it is the last hour, & as you have heard, that \f 1 \fr 2.18 \ft \+it c ὁ ἀντίχριςος.\+it*\f* Antichrist cometh: now there are become many Antichrists, whereby we know, that it is the last hour. \ef - \fr 2.18 \fq Many Antichrists. \ft ¹ \+it The holy Apostle S. John\+it* (saith S. Cyprian) \+it did not put a difference betwixt one heresy or schism and another, nor meant any sort that specially separated themselves, but generally called all without exception,\+it* Antichrists, \+it that were adversaries to the Church, or were gone out from the same\+it*. And a little after, \+it It is evident that all be here called Antichrists, that have severed themselves from the charity and unity of the Catholic Church\+it*. So writeth he \+it ep. 76. nu. 1 ad Magnum\+it*. Whereby, we may learn, that all Heretics, or rather Arch-heretics be properly the precursors of the one and special Antichrist, which is to come at the last end of the world, & which is called here immediately before, 'ὁ ἀντίχριστος', that peculiar and singular Antichrist. \fq ¹ \ft All Heretics are Antichrists, the forerunners of the great Antichrist.\ef*
\v 19 They went out from us; but \f 1 \fr 2.19 \ft They were of us for the time, that is, of and in the Church: otherwise they could not have gone out. But they were not of the constant sort, or of the elect & predestinate: for then they had tarried within, or returned before their death.\f* they were not of us. For if they had been of us, they would surely have remained with us: but that they may be manifest that they are not all of us. \ef - \fr 2.19 \fq They went out from us. \ft ¹ An evident note and mark, whereby to convince all Heretics and false teachers, to wit, that being once of the common Catholic Christian fellowship, they forsook it, and went out from the same. Simon Magus, Nicolas the Deacon, Hymenæus, Alexander, Philetus, Arius, Macedonius, Pelagius, Nestorius, Eutyches, Luther, Calvin, and the like, were of the common society of all us that be Christian Catholics, they went out from us whom they saw to live in unity of faith and religion together, and made themselves new conventicles. therefore they were (as the Apostle here sheweth) Antichrists, and we and all that abide in the ancient fellowship of Christian religion, that went not out of their fellowship, in which we never were, nor out of any other society of known Christians, cannot be schismatics or Heretics, but must needs be true Christian Catholic men. ² Let our adversaries tell us, out of what Church we ever departed, when, and where, and under what persons it was that we revolted, as we can tell them the year, the places, the Ringleaders of their revolt. \fq ¹ \ft The mark of all heretics is, their going out of the Catholic society. \fq ² \ft The Catholics cannot be proved to have gone out.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.19 \fq They were not of us. \ft ¹ He meaneth not, that Heretics were not, or could not be in or of the Church, because they went out or fell into their heresy or schism: but partly that many of them which afterward fall out, though they were before with the rest, and partakers of all the Sacraments with other their fellows, yet indeed were of naughty life and conscience when they were within. and so being rather as ill humors and superfluous excrements, than true and lively parts of the body, after a sort may be said not to have been of the body at all. So S. Augustine expoundeth these words in his commentary upon this place. \+it tract. 3.\+it* but else-where, more agreeably as it seemeth, that the Apostle meaneth, that such as will not tarry in the Church, but finally forsake it to the end, in the prescience of God, and in respect of the small benefit they shall have by their temporal small abode there, be not of or in the Church, though according to this present state, they are truly members thereof. \+it Li. de corrept. & gr. c. 9 & de dono persever. c. 8.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft How Heretics are of the Church, before they fall.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.19 \fq That they may be manifest. \ft ¹ God permitteth heresy to be, that such as be permanent, constant, and chosen members and children of the Catholic Church, only known to God before, may now also be made manifest to the world, by their constant remaining in the \+sc Church\+sc*, when the wind and blast of every heresy or tentation driveth out the other light and unstable persons. \fq ¹ \ft By heresies constant Catholics are known.\ef*
\v 20 But you have \f 1 \fr 2.20 \ft \+it c Χρίσμα Chrisma. whereof Christ & Christians\+it*\f* the unction from the Holy one, and know all things. \ef - \fr 2.20 \fq Know all things. \ft ¹ They that abide in the unity of Christ's Church, have \+it the unction\+it*, that is, the Holy Ghost, who teacheth all truth. Not that every member or man thereof hath all knowledge in himself personally, but that every one which is of that happy society to which Christ promised and gave the Holy Ghost, is partaker of all other men's gifts and graces in the same Holy Spirit, to his salvation. Neither need any to seek truth at Heretics' hands or others that be gone out, when it is within themselves and only within themselves in God's Church. \+it If thou love unity\+it* (saith S. Augustine) \+it for thee also hath he, whosoever hath any thing in it, take away envy, it is thine which I have, it is mine which thou hast. &c.\+it* Tract. 32 in Evan. Ioan. \fq ¹ \ft Every good Catholic is sufficiently taught by the Church to salvation.\ef*
\v 21 I have not written to you as to them that know not the truth, but as to them that know it: and that no lie is of the truth.
\v 22 Who is a liar, but he which denieth that \sc Jesus\sc* is Christ? This is Antichrist which denieth the Father and the Son.
\v 23 Every one that denieth the Son, neither hath he the Father. He that confesseth the Son, hath the Father also.
\v 24 You, that which you have \f 1 \fr 2.24 \ft Keep that firmly & constantly which you have heard even from the beginning, by the mouth of the Apostles; & not that only which you have received by writing.\f* heard from the beginning, let it abide in you. If that abide in you which you have heard from the beginning, you also shall abide in the Son & the Father.
\v 25 And this is the promise which he promised us, life everlasting.
\v 26 These things have I written to you concerning them that seduce you.
\v 27 And you, the unction which you have received from him, let it abide in you. And you have no need that any man teach you: but as his unction teacheth you of all things, and it is true, and it is no lie. And as it hath taught you, abide \f 1 \fr 2.27 \ft c or, \+it in it.\+it*\f* in him.
\v 28 And now little children abide in him: that when he shall appear, we may have confidence, and not be confounded of him in his coming.
\v 29 If you know that he is just, know ye that every one also \f 1 \fr 2.29 \ft We see it is Apostolical doctrine, that men may do or work justice, and that so doing they be just by their works proceeding of God's grace, & not by faith or imputation only.\f* which doeth justice, is born of him.
\c 3
\cd It is not for the sons of God, to sin mortally, but for the sons of the Devil, whereby they are known one from another, & not by only faith. 11. True faith is, that we also love our brethren, giving both our life and substance for them. 19. Such unfeigned love may have great confidence before God. 23. because the keeping of his commandments doth much please him, which consist in faith and charity.
\p
\v 1 \sc See\sc* what manner of charity the Father hath given us, that we should be named and be \f 1 \fr 3.1 \ft Not by nature, as Christ is: but by grace and adoption.\f* the sons of God. For this cause the world doth not know us, because it hath not known him.
\v 2 My dearest, now we are the sons of God; & it hath not yet appeared what we shall be. We know that when he shall appear, we shall be like to him: because we shall \f 1 \fr 3.2 \ft c How we shall see God & be like unto him in the next life, see S. Augustine. \+it ep. 111. 112. & li. 12. de civit. Dei c. 29.\+it*\f* see him as he is.
\v 3 And every one that hath this hope in him, \f 1 \fr 3.3 \ft This teacheth us that man sanctifieth himself by his free-will working together with God's grace. S. Augustine \+it upon this place.\+it*\f* sanctifieth himself, as he also is holy.
\v 4 Every one that committeth sin, committeth also iniquity: and sin is iniquity. \ef - \fr 3.4 \fq Sin is iniquity. \ft Iniquity is not taken here for wickedness, as it is commonly used both in Latin and in our language, as is plain by the Greek word \+it ἀνομία\+it*, signifying nothing else but a swarving or declining from the straight line of the law of God or nature. ¹ So that the Apostle meaneth, that every sin is an obliquity or defect from the rule of the law: but not contrary, that every such swarving from the law, should be properly a sin, as the Heretics do untruly gather, to prove that concupiscence remaining after Baptism is a very sin, though we never give our consent unto it. And though in the 5. chapter following vers. 17. the Apostle turn the speech, affirming every iniquity to be a sin, yet there the Greek word is not the same as before, \+it ἀνομία\+it*, but \+it ἀδικία\+it*. by which it is plain that there he meaneth by \+it iniquity\+it*, man's actual and proper transgression which must needs be a sin. See S. Augustine \+it cont. Iulian. li. 5. c. 3. S. Ambr. li. de Apologia David c. 13.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft concupiscence remaining after Baptism is no sin, without consent.\ef*
\v 5 And \x - \xt Es. 53, 4.\x* you know that he appeared to take away our sins: \x - \xt 1. Pet. 2, 24.\x* and sin in him there is none.
\v 6 Every one that abideth in him, sinneth not: and every one that sinneth, hath not seen him, nor known him. \ef - \fr 3.6 \fq Sinneth not. \ft ¹ Iovinian & Pelagius falsely (as Heretics use to do) argued upon these words, and those that follow vers. 9: the one, that the baptized could sin no more: the other, that no man being or remaining just could sin. But among many good senses given of this place, this seemeth most agreeable, that the Apostle should say, that mortal sin doth not consist together with the grace of God, & therefore cannot be committed by a man continuing the son of God ². And so is the like speech in the 9. verse following to be taken. See S. Hierom \+it li. 2. cont. Iovinianum c. 1.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Heretical exposition of Scriptures. \fq ² \ft No man in grace sinneth mortally.\ef*
\v 7 Little children, let no man seduce you. He that doeth justice, is just: even as he also is just. \ef - \fr 3.7 \fq He that doeth justice. \ft ¹ He doth inculcate this often, that man's true justice or righteousness consisteth in doing or working justice, and that so he is just, and biddeth them not to be seduced by Heretics, in this point. \fq ¹ \ft True justice.\ef*
\v 8 \x - \xt Io. 8, 44.\x* He that committeth sin, is of the devil: because the devil sinneth from the beginning. For this, appeared the Son of God, that he might dissolve the works of the devil. \ef - \fr 3.8 \fq Sinneth from the beginning. \ft The Devil was created holy and in grace, and not in sin: but he fell of his own free will from God. Therefore these words, \+it from the beginning\+it*, may be interpreted thus, from the beginning of sin, and so the Apostle will say, The Devil committed the first sin. ¹ So S. Augustine \+it li. 11 de civ. Dei c. 15.\+it* expoundeth it. The most simple meaning seemeth to be, that he sinned from the beginning of the world, not taking the beginning precisely for the first instant or moment of the creation, but straight upon the beginning, as it must needs also be taken in S. John's Gospel c. 8. 44. \fq ¹ \ft How the Devil sinned from the beginning.\ef*
\v 9 Every one that is born of God, committeth not sin: because his seed abideth in him, and he cannot sin because he is born of God.
\v 10 In this are the children of God manifest, and the children of the devil. Every one that is not just, is not of God, and he that loveth not his brother.
\v 11 \x - \xt Io. 13. 15.\x* Because this is the annunciation, which you have heard from the beginning, \f 2 \fr 3.11 \ft The Epistle for S. Polycarpus, \+it Ian. 26.\+it*\f* That you love one another.
\v 12 Not as \x - \xt Gen. 4, 8.\x* Cain, who was of the wicked, and killed his brother. And for what cause killed he him? Because his works were wicked: but his brother's, just.
\v 13 \f 1 \fr 3.13 \ft The Epistle upon the 2. Sunday after Pentecost.\f* Marvel not, brethren, if the world hate you.
\v 14 We know that we are translated from death to life, because we love the brethren. He that loveth not, abideth in death.
\v 15 Whosoever hateth his brother, is a murderer. And you know that no murderer hath life everlasting abiding in himself.
\v 16 \x - \xt Io. 15, 13.\x* In this we have known the charity of God, because he hath yielded his life for us: and we ought to yield our lives for the brethren.
\v 17 \x - \xt Ia. 2, 15.\x* He that shall have the substance of the world, and shall \f 2 \fr 3.17 \ft Every man is bound to give alms according to his ability, when he seeth his brother in great necessity.\f* see his brother have need, and shall shut his bowels from him: how doth the charity of God abide in him?
\v 18 My little children, let us not love in word, nor in tongue, but in deed and truth.
\v 19 In this we know that we are of the truth: and in his sight we shall persuade our hearts.
\v 20 For if our heart do reprehend us, God is greater than our heart, and knoweth all things.
\v 21 My dearest, if our heart do not reprehend us, we have confidence toward God.
\v 22 And \x - \xt Mat. 21. Io. 14. 1. Io. 5.\x* whatsoever we shall ask, we shall receive of him: because we keep his commandments, and do those things which are pleasing before him. \ef - \fr 3.22 \fq we shall receive, because. \ft ¹ Let the Protestants be ashamed to say, that we obtain all of God by only faith, the Apostle here attributing it to the keeping of God's commandments. Note here also that God's commandments are not impossible to be kept, but were then, and are now observed of good men. \fq ¹ \ft Not only faith.\ef*
\v 23 And \x - \xt Io. 17, 3. 13, 34.\x* this is his commandment, that we believe in the name of his Son \sc Jesus\sc* Christ: and \f 2 \fr 3.23 \ft Lest any man should think by the words next before, only faith in Christ to be commanded or to please God, he addeth to faith, the commandment of charity or love of our neighbour.\f* love one another, as he hath given commandment unto us.
\v 24 And \x - \xt Io. 14, 23.\x* he that keepeth his commandments, abideth in him, and he in him. And in this we know that he abideth in us, by the Spirit which he hath given us.
\c 4
\cd We may not believe all that boast of the spirit, but try them, whether they teach Catholic articles of the faith (namely the incarnation of Christ:) whether their doctrine be not worldly, and themselves disobedient hearers of the Apostles. 7. We must love one another, considering the exceeding love of God in sending his son to save us. 17. An argument of perfect charity is, if we have nothing in our conscience to fear in the day of judgement. 19. And an argument that we love God is, if we love our brethren.
\p
\v 1 MY dearest, believe not every spirit, but prove the spirits if they be of God: because, many false prophets are gone out into the world. \ef - \fr 4.1 \fq Believe not every spirit. \ft ¹ That is, Receive not every doctrine of such as boast themselves to have the spirit. For there be many false prophets, that is to say, Heretics, which shall go out of the Church, and challenge the spirit, and vaunt of God's word, Scripture, and Gospel, which indeed be seducers. \fq ¹ \ft Heretical boasting of the spirit.\ef* \ef - \fr 4.1 \fq Prove the spirits. \ft ¹ It is not meant by this place, as the Protestants would have it, that every particular person should of himself examine, try, or judge who is a true or false Doctor, and which is true or false doctrine. But the Apostle here would every one to discern these diversities of spirits, by taking knowledge of them to whom God hath given the gift of discerning spirits and doctrines (which S. Paul expressly saith is given but to some, and not to every one, 1 Cor. 12.) and by obeying the Church of God, to whom Christ hath given ² the Spirit of truth. And this is only the sure way to prove the spirits and doctrines of these days. And all they that would bring us from our Pastor's and the Church's judgement, to our own private trial, seek nothing else but to drive us to miserable uncertaintly in all our belief. as ³ Calvin doth, who upon this place saith, that private men may examine the general Councels' doctrines. \fq ¹ \ft The Church only, not every private man, hath to prove and discern spirits. \fq ² \ft \+it Io. 14, 16.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Calvin.\ef*
\v 2 In this is the spirit of God known. Every spirit that confesseth \sc Jesus\sc* Christ to have come in flesh, is of God: \ef - \fr 4.2 \fq Every spirit that confesseth. \ft ¹ The Apostle speaketh according to that time, and for that part of Christian doctrine which then was specially to be confessed, taught, & maintained against certain wicked Heretics, Cerinthus, Ebion, and the like, that taught wickedly against the person and both natures of Christ \+sc Jesus\+sc*. The Apostle therefore giveth the faithful people this token to know the true teachers of those days from the false. Not that this mark would serve for all times, or in case of all other false doctrines, but that it was then a necessary note. As if a good Catholic writer, Pastor, or parents would warn all theirs, now in these days, to give ear only to such teachers as acknowledge Christ our Saviour to be really present, and sacrificed in the B. Mass, & that all such are true preachers and of God, the rest to be of the Devil, or to be counted the spirit of Antichrist. Which spirit of Antichrist (he saith) was come even then, and is no doubt much more now in all Heretics, all being precursors of that great Antichrist which shall come towards the later end. \fq ¹ \ft To confess or deny any article which the Cath. Church teacheth, is at all times a certain mark of Catholic or Heretic.\ef*
\v 3 and every spirit that dissolveth \sc Jesus\sc*, is not of God: and this is \f 1 \fr 4.3 \ft \+it c τὸ τοῦ ἀντίχριςτου.\+it*\f* Antichrist, of whom you have heard that he cometh, and now he is in the world. \ef - \fr 4.3 \fq That dissolveth. \ft ¹ To dissolve, loose, or separate \+sc Jesus\+sc* a-sunder, was proper to all those old Heretics that taught either against his Divinity, or Humanity, or the Unity of his person, being of two natures, as Cerinthus, Ebion, Nestorius, Eutyches, Manes or Manichæus, Cerdon, Apelles, Apollinaris and the like. ² And this is one place by which we may see that the common Greek copies be not ever authentical, and that our old approved translation may not always be examined by the Greek that now is, which the Protestants only follow: but that it is to be presupposed, when our old Latin text differeth plainly from the Greek, that in old time either all or the more approved Greek reading was otherwise, and that often the said Greek was corrupted then or since by Heretics or otherwise. For of the Greeks, S. Irenæus \+it li. 3. c. 18\+it*: among the Latin Fathers, S. Augustine \+it tract. 6. in fine, S. Leo ep. 10. c. 5,\+it* and Venerable Bede did read as we do. and this reading maketh more against the said Heretics, than that which the common Greek now hath, to wit, \+it Every spirit that confesseth not Christ to have come in flesh, is not of God\+it*. Which is also in effect said before vers. 2. And that therefore it was corrupted and altered by Heretics, see the words of Socrates also a Greek writer, very agreeable to this purpose. \+it Nestorius\+it* (saith he ³ ) \+it being eloquent by nature\+it*, which is often in Heretics, \+it accounted himself therefore learned, & disdained to study the old interpreters, counting himself better than them all: being ignorant that in S. John's Catholic epistle the old\+it* (Greek) \+it copies had, \+sc Every One That Dissolveth Jesus, Is Not Of God\+sc*\+it*. So saith he, adding moreover that such as would separate the divinity from the dispensation of Christ's humanity, took out of the old copies this sense. for which the old expositors noted that these which would loose \+sc Jesus\+sc*, had corrupted this Epistle. See also the Tripartite li. 12. c. 4. \fq ¹ \ft Many old heresies that dissolved Christ. \fq ² \ft The Greek text corrupted by old heretics. \fq ³ \ft \+it li. 7. c. 32.\+it*\ef*
\v 4 You are of God, little children, and have overcome him. Because greater is he that is in you, than he that is in the world.
\v 5 They are of the world: therefore of the world they speak, and the world heareth them.
\v 6 We are of God. \x - \xt Io. 8. 47. 10, 27.\x* He that knoweth God, heareth us. He that is not of God, heareth us not. In this we know the spirit of truth, and the spirit of error. \ef - \fr 4.6 \fq In this we know. \ft ¹ This is the most sure & general mark to know the true spirits and prophets from the false: that those which be of God, will hear and obey their Apostles and lawful pastors succeeding the Apostles, and submit themselves to the Church of God: the other, that be not of God, will not hear either Apostle, pastor, or Church, but he their own judges. \fq ¹ \ft A sure mark of true or false teachers.\ef*
\v 7 My dearest, let us love one another: because charity is of God. And every one that loveth, is born of God, & knoweth God.
\v 8 \f 1 \fr 4.8 \ft The Epistle upon the first Sunday after Pentecost.\f* He that loveth not, knoweth not God: because God is charity.
\v 9 \x - \xt Io. 3, 16.\x* In this hath the charity of God appeared in us, because God hath sent his only-begotten Son into the world, that we may live by him.
\v 10 In this is charity: not as though we have loved him, but because he hath loved us, and sent his Son a propitiation for our sins.
\v 11 My dearest, if God hath so loved us, we also ought to love one another.
\v 12 \x - \xt Io. 1, 18. 1. Tim. 6, 16.\x* God \f 2 \fr 4.12 \ft No man in this life, nor with corporal eyes, can see the proper essence or substance of the Deity. See S. August. ad Paulin. de videndo Deo. cp. 112.\f* no man hath seen at any time. If we love one another, God abideth in us, and his charity in us is perfited.
\v 13 In this we know that we abide in him, and he in us: because he of his Spirit hath given to us.
\v 14 And we have seen, and do testify, that the Father hath sent his Son the Saviour of the world.
\v 15 Whosoever shall confess that \sc Jesus\sc* is the Son of God, God abideth in him, and he in God.
\v 16 And we have known and have believed the charity, which God hath in us. God is charity: and he that abideth in charity, abideth in God, and God in him.
\v 17 In this is charity perfited with us, that we may have confidence in the day of judgement: because as he is, we also are in the world. \ef - \fr 4.17 \fq That we may have confidence. \ft ¹ Confidence called in Latin \+it Fiducia\+it*, is neither all one with faith, nor a persuasion infallible that maketh a man no less secure and certain of his salvation, than of the things that we are bound to believe, as the Protestants falsely teach: but it is only a hope well corroborated, confirmed, and strengthened upon the promises and grace of God, and the party's merits. And the words both following & going before, prove also evidently against the Protestants, that our confidence and hope in the day of judgement dependeth not only upon our apprehension of Christ's merits by faith, or upon his grace and mercy, but also upon our conformity to Christ in this life, in charity and good works. And that is the doctrine of S. Peter ² when he said, \+it Labour, that by good works you may make sure your vocation and election\+it*. and S. Paul's meaning ³, when he said, \+it I have fought a good fight, there is laid up for me a crown of justice, which our Lord will render to me in that day, a just judge\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft Against the Protestants' special faith and presumptuous security of salvation. \fq ² \ft \+it 2. Pet. 1, 10.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it 2. Tim. 4, 7.\+it*\ef*
\v 18 Fear is not in charity: but perfect charity casteth out fear, because fear hath painfulness. And he that feareth, is not perfect in charity. \ef - \fr 4.18 \fq Fear is not in charity. \ft ¹ The Heretics very falsely understand this place so, that Christian godly men ought to have no doubt, mistrust, or fear of hell and damnation. Which is most evidently against the Scriptures, commending every-where unto us the awe and fear of God and his judgements. \+it Fear him\+it* (saith our Saviour Mat. 10) \+it that can cast body and soul into hell\+it*. And Psal. 118. \+it Pierce my flesh with thy fear\+it*. Which fear of God's judgements caused S. Paul ² & all good men to chastise their bodies, lest they should be reprobate and damned. And the wise man for this cause affirmeth him to be happy, ³ \+it that is ever fearful\+it*. And holy Job saith, ⁴ \+it I feared all my works\+it*. And the Apostle, ⁵ \+it with fear and trembling work your salvation\+it*. Which kind of fear is even in the justest men and most full of charity, consisting well with the same virtue, and is called \+it Filialis timor\+it*, because it is such as the good child ought to have toward his father. \fq ¹ \ft The fear of God in just men, consisteth with charity. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Cor. 9.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Prov. 28.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Iob c. 9.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Phil. 2.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 4.18 \ft ¹ But there is a kind of fear which standeth not with charity, and is clean against hope also, that which bringeth such perplexity and anxiety of conscience, that it induceth a man to mistrust or despair of God's mercies. That servile fear also which maketh a man often to leave sinning and to do the external works of justice, not for any love or delight he hath in God or his laws, but only for fear of damnation, ² though it be not ill in it-self, but very profitable, as that which helpeth toward the love of God, yet it standeth not with charity neither, but is daily more and more lessened, and at length quite driven out by charity. Of these kind of fears then the Apostle speaketh, and (as some expound) of the fear of men also, of which our Saviour saith, \+it Fear not them that kill the body\+it*. ³ \fq ¹ \ft What fear agreeth not with charity. \fq ² \ft Servile fear is not ill. \fq ³ \ft \+it Mat. 10.\+it*\ef*
\v 19 Let us therefore love God, because God first hath loved us.
\v 20 If any man shall say, that I love God; and hateth his brother, he is a liar. For he that loveth not his brother whom he seeth, God whom he seeth not, how can he love?
\v 21 \x - \xt Io. 13, 34. 15, 12.\x* And this commandment we have from God: that he which loveth God, love also his brother.
\c 5
\cd They that love God, must love his natural son JESUS, and his sons by adoption, and keep his commandments, which to the regenerate are light. 4. But not, unless they continue in the Catholic faith, namely of this article, that JESUS is the son of God, and therefore able to give us life everlasting, 14. and all our petitions, 16. and our prayers for all our brethren that sin not unto death, dying in their mortal sins by impenitence. Last of all, he warneth them not to communicate with idols.
\p
\v 1 \sc Whosoever\sc* believeth that \sc Jesus\sc* is Christ, is born of God. And every one that loveth him which begat, loveth him also which was born of him.
\v 2 In this we know that we love the children of God: when as we love God, and keep his commandments.
\v 3 For this is the charity of God, that we keep his commandments: \x - \xt Mat. 11, 30.\x* and his commandments are not heavy. \ef - \fr 5.3 \fq His commandments are not heavy. \ft ¹ How can the Protestants say that God's commandments cannot possibly be fulfilled or kept in this life, seeing the Apostle saith, \+it they be not heavy\+it*: and Christ saith, ² \+it his yoke is sweet, and his burden light\+it*? See for the full understanding of this place. S. Augustine \+it de perfectione justitiæ c. 10.\+it* ³ The Heretics in favour of their foresaid error, rather translate, \+it His commandments are not\+it* ⁴ \+it grievous\+it*: than, \+it are not heavy\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The commandments possible to be kept. \fq ² \ft \+it Mat. 11, 30.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Heret. translation. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it * βαρεῖαι\+it*\ef*
\v 4 \f 1 \fr 5.4 \ft The Epistle \+it upon Dominica in albis\+it* or Low Sunday.\f* Because all that is born of God, overcometh the world: And this is the victory which overcometh the world, our faith.
\v 5 Who is he \x - \xt 1. Cor. 15, 57.\x* that overcometh the world, but he that believeth that \sc Jesus\sc* is the Son of God?
\v 6 This is he that came by water & blood \sc Jesus\sc* Christ: not in water only, but in water and blood. And it is the Spirit which testifieth, that Christ is the truth.
\v 7 For there be three which give testimony in heaven, the Father, the Word, and the Holy Ghost. And these three be one. \ef - \fr 5.7 \fq Three which give testimony. \ft ¹ An express place for the distinction of three Persons, & the unity of nature and essence in the B. Trinity; against the Arians and other like Heretics, who have in diverse ages found themselves so pressed with these plain Scriptures, that they have (as it is thought) altered and corrupted ² the text both in Greek and Latin many ways: even as the Protestants handle those texts that make against them. But because we are not now troubled with Arianism so much as with Calvinism, we need not stand upon the variety of readings or expositions of this passage. See S. Hierom in his epistle put before the 7 Canonical or Catholic Epistles. \fq ¹ \ft Three persons & one substance in the B. Trinity. \fq ² \ft The Arians corrupt the text of Scripture.\ef*
\v 8 And there be three which give testimony in earth: the spirit, water, and blood: and these three be one.
\v 9 If we receive the testimony of men, the testimony of God is greater. Because this is the testimony of God which is greater, that he hath testified of his Son.
\v 10 \x - \xt Io. 3, 36.\x* He that believeth in the Son of God, hath the testimony of God in himself. He that believeth not the Son, maketh him a liar: because he believeth not in the testimony which God hath testified of his Son.
\v 11 And this is the testimony, that God hath given us life everlasting. And this life is in his Son.
\v 12 He that hath the Son, hath life. He that hath not the Son of God, hath not life.
\v 13 These things I write to you, that you may know that you have eternal life which believe in the name of the Son of God.
\v 14 And this is the confidence which we have toward him: that, \x - \xt Mt. 7, 7. 21, 22. 1. Io. 3, 22.\x* whatsoever we shall ask according to his will, he heareth us.
\v 15 And \f 1 \fr 5.15 \ft c or, \+it if we know\+it*\f* we know that he heareth us whatsoever we shall ask: we know that we have the petitions which we request of him.
\v 16 He that knoweth his brother to sin a sin not to death, let him ask, and life shall be given him, sinning not to death. There is a sin to death: for that I say not that any man ask. \ef - \fr 5.16 \fq A sin to death. \ft ¹ A sin to death is another thing than a mortal sin. for it is that mortal sin only, whereof a man is never penitent before his death, or in which he continueth till death, and dieth in it. \+it I affirm\+it* (saith S. Augustine de correp. & grat. c. 12) \+it that a sin to death is to leave faith working by charity even till death\+it*, so likewise in the words before, \+it a sin not to death\+it*, is not that which we call a venial sin, but any that a man committeth and continueth not therein till death. \fq ¹ \ft What is a sin to death.\ef* \ef - \fr 5.16 \fq For that I say not. \ft If the sin to death whereof he speaketh, be the sin wherein a man dieth without repentance, according to S. Augustine's words before rehearsed: then the prayer which he speaketh of, must needs be ¹ prayer for the dead. because he speaketh of praying, or not praying, for them that died in deadly sin, exhorting us to pray, and encouraging us to do it with confidence to be heard, if we pray for them that departed this life not in deadly sin: and contrarywise in manner dissuading & discouraging us from praying for such as continued in wickedness even till their life's end. And S. Augustine setteth down the Church's practice agreeable to the Apostle's meaning, li. 21 c. 24 de Civit. Dei. \+it If there be any\+it* (saith he) \+it that persist till death in impenitency of heart, doth the Church now pray for them, that is, for the souls of them that so are departed?\+it* So saith he. ² And this is the cause, that \+it Concilium Bracharense primum cap. 34.\+it* forbiddeth to pray for such as die in desperation, or kill themselves: and the reason, why the Church forbeareth to pray for Heretics that die in their heresy, or maintain heresy unto death and by their death. \fq ¹ \ft Prayer for the dead. \fq ² \ft Some of the dead may not be prayed for.\ef* \ef - \fr 5.16 \ft ¹ And that the place is most properly or only meant of praying for the departed, this convinceth, that neither the Church nor any man is dehorted here from praying for any sinner yet living. nor for the remission of any sin in this life: all sins (of what sort soever) being pardonable, so long as the committers of them be in case and state to repent: as they be, so long as they be in this world. And we see that the Church prayeth, and is often heard, for Heretics, Jews, Turks, Apostates, and what other infidels or ill men soever, during their lives. ² And it is great blasphemy that the Calvinists utter upon this place: to wit, that Apostasy and certain other sins of the reprobate, cannot be forgiven at all in this life. Which they hold, only to avoid the sequel of praying for the dead upon these words of S. John. besides that they must take upon them presumptuously, to know and discern of God's secrets, who be reprobate, and who be not, and according to that, pray for some, and not other-some. all which is most wicked and absurd presumption. As for their allegation, that S. Jeremie the Prophet was forbidden to pray for the Jews, and warned that he should not be heard, Chap. 7. 11. 14: there is great difference. first, he had a revelation by the words of God, that they would continue in their wickedness, as we have not of any certain person, whereof S. John here speaketh. Secondly, Jeremie was not forbidden to pray for the remission of their sins, nor had denial to be heard therein for any man's particular case, whereof the Apostle here speaketh: but he was told that they should not escape the temporal punishment and affliction which he had designed for them, and that he would not hear him therein. \fq ¹ \ft It is proved that the Apostle speaketh of praying for the dead. \fq ² \ft The Calvinists' blasphemy, to avoid this sense of the Apostle.\ef*
\v 17 All \f 1 \fr 5.17 \ft \+it c ἀδικία\+it*\f* iniquity, is sin. And there is a sin \f 2 \fr 5.17 \fq to death \ft \+it not to death.\+it*\f* to death.
\v 18 We know that every one which is born of God, sinneth not: but the generation of God preserveth him, and the wicked one toucheth him not.
\v 19 We know that we are of God, and the whole world is set in wickedness.
\v 20 And we know that the Son of God cometh: and he \x - \xt Luc. 24, 45.\x* hath given us understanding, that we may know the true God, & may be in his true Son, This is the true God, & life everlasting.
\v 21 My little children, keep yourselves from idols \f 1 \fr 5.21 \ft \+it ἀπὸ τῶν ἐιδώλων.\+it*\f*. Amen. \ef - \fr 5.21 \fq From idols. \ft ¹ It is so known a treachery of Heretics to translate \+it idola\+it* images (as here and in a number of places, specially of the English Bible printed the year 1562) that we need not much to stand upon it. As this also is seen to all the world, that they do it of purpose to seduce the poor ignorant people, and to make them think, that whatsoever in the Scriptures is spoken against the idols of the Gentiles (which the Prophet called ² \+it Simulacra Gentium\+it* ) is meant of pictures, sacred images, & holy memories of Christ and his Saints. ³ Against such seducers the second sacred Councel of Nice ⁴, called the seventh Synod, decreeth thus Act. 4. pag. 122. \+it Quicunque sententias sacræ scripturæ de Idolis, contra venerandas imagines adducunt, anathema. Qui venerandas imagines idola appellant, anathema. Qui dicunt quod Christiani adorant imagines ut Deos, anathema\+it*. that is, \+it Anathema to all them that bring the sentences of holy Scripture touching Idols, against the venerable images. Anathema to them that call the venerable images, idols. Anathema to them that say, Christians adore images as gods.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Heret. translation against sacred images. \fq ² \ft \+it Psal. 113.\+it* \fq ³ \ft The 2. Councel of Nice pronounceth anathema, that is, a curse against the Calvinists. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Edit. Colon. an. 1567.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 5.21 \ft Now in their later translations the Heretics perceiving that the world seeth their unhonest dealing, corrected themselves in some places, and in this place have put, \+it idols\+it*, in the text: but to give the people a watchword that the Church's images are to be comprised in the word, \+it idols\+it*, ¹ they have put, \+it images\+it*, in the margent. But concerning this matter, it is most evident that neither every idol is an image, not every image an idol: ² and that, howsoever the origin or etymology of the word, \+it idol\+it*, may be taken in the Greek, yet both the words and the things be in truth and by the use of all tongues, far differing. The great dragon that the Babylonians adored ( \+it Dan. 14\+it* ) was an idol, but not an image: the Cherubins in Salomon's temple were images, but not idols: and the face of the Queen in her coin or else-where, as Cæsar's face upon the coin that Christ called for, is an image, but not an idol. and the Heretics dare not translate that text of Scripture thus, \+it whose idol is this superscription?\+it* nor call the Queen's image, the idol of the Queen: nor Christ, the idol of his father: nor woman, the idol of the man: nor man, the idol of God. All which in Scripture be named images for all that, and be so indeed, and not idols. which convinceth, that the Heretics be false & corrupt translators in this place and other the like, confounding these two words as if they were all one. \fq ¹ \ft \+it * The Bible of the year 1577.\+it* \fq ² \ft The great difference of idol & image.\ef* \ef - \fr 5.21 \ft ¹ But as for the having of images or portraits of holy things, not only in private houses, but also in Churches, God himself doth warrant us, who ² commanded even the Jews themselves (a people most prone to idolatry, and that after he had given them a special precept of not having, making, or worshipping of idols) to make the images of Angels (the Cherubins) and that in the sovereign holiest places of adoration that was in the Temple, & about the Ark. Yea and in respect of which sacred images partly, they did (as S. Hierom saith \+it ep. 17 c. 3\+it* ) so great reverence to the holy place called \+it Sancta sanctorum\+it*. If they then were warranted and commanded to make and have in so great reverence the images of mere spirits or Angels, whose natural shape could not be expressed: how much more may we Christians have and reverence the images of Christ, his B. mother, the Apostles, and other Saints, being men, whose shape may be expressed? So doth the said ³ Nicene Councel argue against the Heretics which at that time were the Adversaries of images. \fq ¹ \ft Sacred images in Churches, by God's own warrant. \fq ² \ft \+it Exod. 25.\+it* \fq ³ \ft The 2. Councel of Nice was gathered against Image breakers.\ef* \ef - \fr 5.21 \ft ¹ \fq ¹ \ft The antiquity of holy images.\ef* \ef - \fr 5.21 \ft And note here, that eight hundred years ago, they were straight counted Heretics, that began to speak against images, and that Councel was called purposely for them, and condemned them for Heretics, & confirmed the former ancient reverence and use of sacred images. which began even in our Saviour's time or little after, when good religious folk for love and reverence made his image, namely the woman that he healed of the bloody flux. which image was also approved by miracles, as the Ecclesiastical history telleth, and namely Eusebius \+it Eccl. hist. li. 7. c. 14.\+it* ¹ who also witnesseth that the images of Peter and Paul were in his days, as you may see also in S. Augustine ( \+it li. d. consens. Evangelist. c. 10\+it* ) that their pictures commonly stood together in Rome even as at this day. Of our Lady's image see S. Gregory \+it li. 7. ep. 5. indict. 2. ad Ianuar. & ep. 53\+it*. ² In whom also ( \+it li. 7. ep. 109\+it* ) you may see the true use of images, & that they are the books of the unlearned, and that the people ought to be instructed and taught the right use of them, even as at this day good Catholic folk do use them to help & increase their devotion, in all Catholic Churches: yea the Lutherans themselves retain them still. S. Damascene wrote three books in defense of sacred images against the foresaid Heretics. \fq ¹ \ft \+it * Loco citato.\+it* \fq ² \ft The use and fruit of holy images.\ef*
